All Kiali tool handlers were passing {{params.GetArguments()}} directly to the Kiali HTTP backend without any upfront parameter validation. This means type mismatches (e.g. the LLM sending {{namespace: 42}} instead of {{namespace: "bookinfo"}}) would either silently fail or produce a cryptic error from the Kiali backend rather than a clear message to the caller.

Apply the {{api.WrapParams}} sticky-error pattern,  already used in the core, Tekton, and other toolsets, to all Kiali handlers. Each handler now validates its required parameters before making the HTTP call to Kiali.

Changes:

* {{get_logs}}: validate {{namespace}}, {{name}}
* {{list_traces}}: validate {{namespace}}, {{serviceName}}
* {{get_pod_performance}}: validate {{namespace}}
* {{get_metrics}}: validate {{resourceType}}, {{namespace}}, {{resourceName}}
* {{manage_istio_config_read}}: validate {{action}}
* {{manage_istio_config}}: validate {{action}}, {{namespace}}, {{group}}, {{version}}, {{kind}}, {{object}}
* {{get_resource_details}}: validate {{resourceType}}
* {{get_mesh_traffic_graph}}: validate {{namespaces}}
* {{get_trace_details}}: validate {{traceId}}

Consistent with the rest of the codebase. Provides early, actionable error messages on type mismatches instead of delegating validation to the upstream Kiali API.

